{"groups":[{"members":["c1","c2"],"primary":"c1","title":"New Jersey Security Deposit Law: deposit cap, interest-bearing account, notice and 30-day return","requirement":"A security deposit may not exceed 1.5 times one month's rent, and yearly increases may not exceed 10% of the current deposit. The deposit must be held in trust in an insured New Jersey interest-bearing account, with interest belonging to the tenant, and returned with interest, less itemized deductions, within 30 days after the tenancy ends, or the landlord is liable for double the amount wrongfully withheld.","key_value":"Max deposit 1.5x one month's rent; yearly increase max 10% of current deposit; max 2.5x monthly rent at lease inception (deposit plus first month); return within 30 days (5 business days if displaced by fire, flood, condemnation or evacuation); double damages for wrongful withholding","status":"in_force","effective_date":null,"end_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 46:8-19 to 46:8-21.5","coverage_conditions":"Most residential rental properties, including mobile homes. A tenant in an owner-occupied two- or three-family dwelling may make the law apply 30 days after sending the landlord a written request.","exemptions":"Owner-occupied two- or three-family dwellings, unless the tenant sends a written request; the law then applies after 30 days. The interest-bearing account provisions do not apply to seasonal rentals, meaning a term of 125 consecutive days or less by a person with a permanent residence elsewhere. The landlord bears the burden of proving seasonal use.","coverage_from":"c1","conflict_note":null}],"dropped":[]}
